AB  - Modern DevOps approaches revolve around Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment (CI/CD) pipelines, which automate software build, test, and deployment tasks. However, configuration mistakes, dependency conflicts, unstable tests, and flawed code modifications often cause pipeline executions to fail, which increases debugging effort, delays releases, and wastes computer resources. Automated failure prediction algorithms are desperately needed as CI/CD systems grow and manual post-failure analysis becomes unfeasible. Using actual GitHub Actions workflow data, this article suggests a machine learning-based framework for forecasting CI/CD pipeline problems. The suggested method incorporates a variety of information sources, such as statistical features taken from execution logs, temporal execution characteristics, workflow metadata, and semantic representations obtained from commit messages using textual feature modeling approaches. Systematic preprocessing and feature engineering are used to convert raw workflow data into structured representations, and resampling techniques are used to overcome the inherent class imbalance between successful and unsuccessful executions. In order to model intricate correlations between pipeline features and execution outcomes with little computational overhead appropriate for real time deployment, an ensemble learning model based on XGBoost is utilized. The suggested framework greatly outperforms metadata only baseline techniques, with an overall prediction accuracy of 94.46%, according to experimental evaluation on over 30,000 workflow executions. Additionally, feature analysis shows that commit-level semantics and log-derived statistics play a significant role in failure prediction. Proactive detection of high-risk pipeline executions is made possible by the suggested framework, which can be easily incorporated into current CI/CD workflows to promote dependable and effective software delivery in real-world DevOps environments.
ER  - 

